let, const, and var

Before ES6, JavaScript mainly used var.

var
var name = "John";
Characteristics
Function scoped
Can be redeclared
Can be reassigned
Hoisted and initialized with undefined
console.log(a); // undefined

var a = 10;

Equivalent to:

var a;

console.log(a);

a = 10;
Problem
if (true) {
    var x = 10;
}

console.log(x); // 10

var ignores block boundaries.

let
let age = 25;
Characteristics
Block scoped
Can be reassigned
Cannot be redeclared in same scope
let x = 10;
x = 20;

Valid.

let x = 10;
let x = 20;

Error.

Block Scope
if (true) {
    let x = 10;
}

console.log(x);

Error.

Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)
console.log(a);

let a = 5;

Error.

Variable exists but cannot be accessed before declaration.

const
const PI = 3.14;
Characteristics
Block scoped
Cannot be reassigned
Must be initialized
const a;

Error.

Important Corner Case
const user = {
    name: "John"
};

user.name = "Mike";

Works.

Because object contents can change.

Only the reference is constant.

user = {};

Error.

Quick Comparison
Feature	var	let	const
Scope	Function	Block	Block
Redeclare	Yes	No	No
Reassign	Yes	Yes	No
Hoisted	Yes	Yes	Yes
TDZ	No	Yes	Yes
2. Arrow Functions

Introduced in ES6.

Traditional:

function add(a, b) {
    return a + b;
}

Arrow:

const add = (a, b) => {
    return a + b;
};
Short Form
const add = (a, b) => a + b;

Implicit return.

One Parameter
const square = x => x * x;
No Parameter
const greet = () => "Hello";
Important Difference: this

Normal function:

const person = {
    name: "John",
    say() {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};

Works.

Arrow function:

const person = {
    name: "John",
    say: () => {
        console.log(this.name);
    }
};

Usually undefined.

Arrow functions don't create their own this.

They inherit from surrounding scope.

When To Use

Good:

arr.map(x => x * 2);

Bad:

const obj = {
    method: () => {}
};

Avoid for object methods.

3. Destructuring

Extract values from arrays or objects.

Array Destructuring
const arr = [10, 20, 30];

const [a, b, c] = arr;

console.log(a);

Output:

10
Skip Values
const [first, , third] = arr;
Default Values
const [a, b = 100] = [10];

Result:

a = 10
b = 100
Rest Operator
const [first, ...rest] = [1,2,3,4];

Result:

first = 1
rest = [2,3,4]
Object Destructuring
const user = {
    name: "John",
    age: 25
};

const { name, age } = user;
Rename
const { name: username } = user;
Default
const { city = "Chennai" } = user;
Nested Destructuring
const user = {
    profile: {
        name: "John"
    }
};

const {
    profile: { name }
} = user;
