7. Promises

Promise represents future completion/failure.

States:

Pending
Fulfilled
Rejected
Creating Promise
const promise = new Promise(
    (resolve, reject) => {
        resolve("Success");
    }
);
Consuming
promise.then(result => {
    console.log(result);
});
Reject
const promise = new Promise(
    (resolve, reject) => {
        reject("Failed");
    }
);
Catch
promise
   .then(data => console.log(data))
   .catch(err => console.log(err));
Finally
promise
   .finally(() => {
      console.log("Done");
   });

Runs regardless of success/failure.

Promise Chaining
fetchUser()
 .then(user => getOrders(user.id))
 .then(orders => getProducts(orders))
 .catch(err => console.log(err));
8. Async/Await

Built on top of Promises.

Makes async code look synchronous.

Basic Example
async function getData() {
    return "Hello";
}

Actually returns:

Promise.resolve("Hello")
await
async function run() {
    const result = await fetchData();

    console.log(result);
}

Execution pauses until promise resolves.

Error Handling
async function run() {
    try {
        const data = await fetchData();
    }
    catch(error) {
        console.log(error);
    }
}
Multiple Await Problem

Bad:

const a = await fetchA();
const b = await fetchB();

Runs sequentially.

Better:

const [a,b] = await Promise.all([
   fetchA(),
   fetchB()
]);

Runs concurrently.

Common Promise Utilities
Promise.all()

Fails if any promise fails.

await Promise.all([
   p1,
   p2,
   p3
]);
Promise.allSettled()

Waits for all.

await Promise.allSettled([
   p1,
   p2
]);
Promise.race()

First completed wins.

await Promise.race([
   p1,
   p2
]);
Promise.any()

First successful wins.

await Promise.any([
   p1,
   p2
]);
9. Modules

Used to split code into files.

Export
Named Export
export const name = "John";

export function greet() {}
Export Together
const name = "John";

function greet() {}

export { name, greet };
Default Export
export default function App() {}

Only one default export per file.

Import
Named Import
import { greet } from "./app.js";
Multiple
import {
   greet,
   name
} from "./app.js";
Alias
import {
   greet as sayHello
} from "./app.js";
Default Import
import App from "./app.js";
Import Everything
import * as Utils from "./app.js";

Usage:

Utils.greet();
Most Important Interview Corner Cases
1. map() vs forEach()
arr.map(...)

Returns new array.

arr.forEach(...)

Returns undefined.

2. == vs ===
5 == "5"

true

5 === "5"

false

Always prefer ===.

3. Spread vs Rest

Spread:

const arr2 = [...arr1];

Expands.

Rest:

const [a, ...rest] = arr;

Collects.

4. Arrow Function Object Return

Wrong:

const fn = () => { name: "John" };

Returns undefined.

Correct:

const fn = () => ({
   name: "John"
});
5. Async Function Always Returns Promise
async function test() {
   return 10;
}

Returns:

Promise { 10 }

not 10.
